[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Reentrancy Attacks and Defense Mechanisms in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reentrancy is one of the most critical security vulnerabilities in smart contract development. It occurs when an external contract is allowed to make a callback before the initial execution is finished, draining funds or corrupting contract state."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Anatomy of a Reentrancy Attack"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a vulnerable vault contract that allows users to deposit and withdraw Ether. If the contract sends Ether to a user before updating their balance, the receiving contract can recursively call the withdraw function again."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"contract VulnerableVault {
    mapping(address => uint256) public balances;

    function deposit() public payable {
        balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;
    }

    function withdraw(uint256 _amount) public {
        require(balances[msg.sender] >= _amount, "Insufficient balance");

        (bool sent, ) = msg.sender.call{value: _amount}("");
        require(sent, "Failed to send Ether");

        balances[msg.sender] -= _amount;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Defense Mechanism 1: Checks-Effects-Interactions Pattern"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary defense against reentrancy is adhering to the Checks-Effects-Interactions design pattern. Always update state variables (effects) before interacting with external contracts (interactions)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"contract SecureVault {
    mapping(address => uint256) public balances;

    function deposit() public payable {
        balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;
    }

    function withdraw(uint256 _amount) public {
        require(balances[msg.sender] >= _amount, "Insufficient balance");

        balances[msg.sender] -= _amount;

        (bool sent, ) = msg.sender.call{value: _amount}("");
        require(sent, "Failed to send Ether");
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Defense Mechanism 2: Reentrancy Guard"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Another robust defense is implementing a mutex lock, commonly known as a ReentrancyGuard. OpenZeppelin provides a standardized implementation of this modifier to prevent recursive calls."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"abstract contract ReentrancyGuard {
    uint256 private constant _NOT_ENTERED = 1;
    uint256 private constant _ENTERED = 2;
    uint256 private _status;

    constructor() {
        _status = _NOT_ENTERED;
    }

    modifier nonReentrant() {
        require(_status != _ENTERED, "ReentrancyGuard: reentrant call");
        _status = _ENTERED;
        _;
        _status = _NOT_ENTERED;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always follow the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern for state modifications."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use audited security libraries like OpenZeppelin ReentrancyGuard for critical functions."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer transfer or transfer data cautiously, remembering that low-level call forwards all remaining gas."}]
